






{"id":"643984b1-5487-4fc8-af95-27133c9aefc6","object":"chat.completion","created":1786440120,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"record_id\":\"CA12_CORE_026\",\"decisions\":[{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_01\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_02\",\"depart_slot\":4,\"charge_blocks\":1},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_02\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_05\",\"depart_slot\":4,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_03\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_01\",\"depart_slot\":8,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_04\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_09\",\"depart_slot\":7,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_05\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_10\",\"depart_slot\":15,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_06\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_02\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_03\",\"depart_slot\":20,\"charge_blocks\":0}]}","reasoning_content":"We are given a public task for a UAV integrated repair optimization. The task is to produce one complete repair, covering every mission exactly once, respecting fixed UAV assignment and mission order, route compatibility, release/due windows, incoming deadhead, charging, SOC/reserve, corridor/pad/charger capacity, closures, and all intervention overrides. Optimize lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\n\nWe need to output exactly a JSON object with the required schema. No Markdown or explanations. No verifier feedback.\n\nThe instance has three UAVs: UAV_01 (fixed_wing_vtol_cargo_uav, capacity 22 kWh, reserve 2.5, initial SOC overridden to 2.731181 kWh, available at slot 4, start VP_04), UAV_02 (capacity 22, reserve 2.5, initial SOC 8.5, available slot 4, start VP_04), UAV_03 (multirotor, capacity 16, reserve 1.5, initial SOC 9.635464, available slot 4, start VP_04).\n\nMissions: \n- MISSION_01: UAV_01, release 4, due 10, from VP_04 to VP_03, payload 0.5 kg. Path options: PATH_02 (0.9 km, 0.1359 kWh, wind 0, travel 2 slots), PATH_06 (1.06 km, 0.170464 kWh, wind 1, travel 2 slots), PATH_07 (1.31 km, 0.19781 kWh, wind 0, travel 2 slots), PATH_11 (1.2 km, 0.192978 kWh, wind 1, travel 2 slots). Note: PATH_06 has wind 1, UAV_01 max wind level is 2, so compatible. Also note corridor closures: COR_02 closed slots 4-27 except maybe we need to check path corridor: PATH_11 uses COR_02? Let's check: PATH_11 corridor_ids [\"COR_02\"], so this path is only available if COR_02 is not closed at departure slot. COR_02 closed from slot 4 onwards, so PATH_11 can't be used because departure slot >=4? Actually corridor closure overrides: closed_corridor_slots includes COR_02 from slot 4 to 27. So if we depart at slot >=4 and use COR_02, it's closed. So PATH_11 is unavailable because it uses COR_02 and that corridor is closed at all possible departure slots (since release slot is 4). So PATH_11 is effectively not usable. So for MISSION_01, available paths: PATH_02 (COR_09), PATH_06 (COR_08), PATH_07 (COR_11). COR_08 has corridor capacity override: capacity 1 at slot 4. So that might be a constraint. \n- MISSION_02: UAV_03, release 4, due 6, from VP_04 to VP_03, payload 0.5 kg, only path PATH_05 (COR_08, distance 1.06, energy 0.132956, wind 1, travel 2 slots). Note: UAV_03 max wind level 1, and this path has wind 1, so compatible. But COR_08 capacity 1 at slot 4, so if we schedule MISSION_02 at slot 4, it uses COR_08. MISSION_01 might also want COR_08 at slot 4? Could conflict.\n- MISSION_03: UAV_01, release 7, due 22, from VP_03 to VP_02, payload 0.5 kg, only path PATH_01 (COR_05, distance 0.8, energy 0.128652, wind 1, travel 1 slot). Max wind for UAV_01 is 2, so OK. COR_05 capacity 2 default.\n- MISSION_04: UAV_03, release 7, due 24, from VP_03 to VP_05, payload 0.5 kg. Path options: PATH_04 (COR_04, distance 4.0, energy 0.50172, wind 1, travel 2 slots), PATH_08 (COR_10, distance 1.0, energy 0.113, wind 0, travel 2 slots), PATH_09 (COR_03, distance 1.0, energy 0.113, wind 0, travel 2 slots). But note: COR_10 closed slots 7-30 (as per intervention closed_corridor_slots: COR_10 slots 7 to 30). So PATH_08 is unavailable because departure slot >=7. So PATH_04 and PATH_09 are available. PATH_09 uses COR_03, which is not closed. So we can choose PATH_04 or PATH_09.\n- MISSION_05: UAV_03, release 15, due 27, from VP_05 to VP_06, payload 0.5 kg, only path PATH_10 (COR_06, distance 3.15, energy 0.35595, wind 0, travel 1 slot).\n- MISSION_06: UAV_02, release 20, due 27, from VP_04 to VP_01, payload 0.5 kg, only path PATH_03 (COR_01, distance 1.0, energy 0.151, wind 0, travel 2 slots).\n\nFixed UAV assignment: each mission has a fixed uav_id. So we cannot change UAV assignment.\n\nMission order: \"FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER\" means for each UAV, the missions assigned to it must be flown in the order given by sequence_index? Actually, the contract says: \"assignment_order_contract\":\"FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER\". The description: \"Respect fixed UAV assignment and mission order\". I interpret that for each UAV, the missions assigned to it (MISSION_01 and MISSION_03 for UAV_01, MISSION_02, MISSION_04, MISSION_05 for UAV_03, MISSION_06 for UAV_02) must be flown in the same relative order as they appear in the list, which is by sequence_index. Sequence indices: MISSION_01: 0, MISSION_02: 0, MISSION_03: 1, MISSION_04: 1, MISSION_05: 2, MISSION_06: 0. So for UAV_01, missions are MISSION_01 (seq 0) then MISSION_03 (seq 1). So order is fixed: MISSION_01 then MISSION_03. For UAV_03: MISSION_02 (0), MISSION_04 (1), MISSION_05 (2). Order fixed: MISSION_02 -> MISSION_04 -> MISSION_05. For UAV_02: only MISSION_06, no ordering issue.\n\nThus we cannot reorder missions within a UAV's sequence.\n\nTurnaround slots: 1. So after a mission arrives at destination, the UAV must spend 1 slot before it can depart for the next mission? Actually, the turnaround is at the destination. The order of events per mission: incoming deadhead (if any) -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. So after the flight, we have a turnaround at the destination that lasts? The \"turnaround_slots\" is 1. That means after the flight arrives, the UAV is on the ground for 1 slot before it can start charging or depart? Actually the description: \"destination turnaround\" occurs after the service flight and reserve check, and before post-service charge. So the turnaround consumes a slot after the arrival. So for scheduling: if a mission departs at slot D, travel time T slots, then it arrives at destination at slot D+T-1? Wait careful: travel slots are the number of slots the flight occupies. Typically, if a flight departs at slot s, it occupies slots s and s+1 if travel_slots=2? But we need to parse: The path options give \"travel_slots\": 2 means flight takes 2 slots. The corridor usage: It likely means the corridor is occupied for the duration. Usually, a flight that travels for T slots departs at slot d, then it is en route during slots d, d+1, ..., d+T-1? Or maybe arrival at the end of slot? Need to clarify from the normative event order: \"incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge\". The departure slot is the slot when the UAV starts its service flight (or incoming deadhead?). It says: \"depart_slot\" is for the mission. The events: incoming deadhead (if any) occurs before the departure? That is, if there is an incoming deadhead from a previous mission's destination to the origin of this mission, that deadhead flight occurs earlier, and then the UAV is available at the origin to start the service flight at release slot or later. The deadhead is registered, but in this instance, all incoming_deadhead are null (so no deadheads). So the UAV just starts at origin at its available slot (or after previous mission turnaround and charging). So the sequence for a mission: (Optional: incoming deadhead, but none here) so the UAV must be at the origin vertiport and available (not in turnaround or charging) at the departure slot. The service flight then occupies the travel slots: if travel_slots = T, and departure slot = s, the flight occurs during slots s, s+1, ..., s+T-1? Typically, the UAV is in flight during those slots and lands at the destination at the end of slot s+T-1, and then turnaround starts? The reserve check is done after the flight, before turnaround? \"Check reserve immediately after incoming-deadhead plus service energy are deducted.\" So after the flight energy is deducted (i.e., the SOC after service flight), we check that the remaining energy is at least reserve_kwh. Then the destination turnaround occupies some slots. The turnaround_slots is 1, meaning the UAV spends one full slot at the destination after landing? Usually, if a flight takes 2 slots (say slots 4 and 5), the UAV arrives at the end of slot 5, then turnaround would be slot 6, and then post-service charge can start in slot 7 and occupy charge_blocks slots. The UAV becomes available for next mission's departure only after turnaround and charging are complete, i.e., at the earliest slot after charge ends. So next departure must be at a slot after that.\n\nSo timeline: For a mission with departure slot s, travel_slots T, turnaround 1, and charge_blocks C:\n- Flight: slots s to s+T-1 (inclusive). Arrival at end of s+T-1.\n- Turnaround: slot s+T (one slot).\n- Charging: if C > 0, charge blocks occupy slots s+T+1 to s+T+C (since each block is one slot of charging). The charging kWh is added at the end of charging? The description: \"At the start of a later mission, all legal post-service charging completed after earlier missions is already included in the UAV's available energy.\" So for the next mission, its available energy at departure includes any charging completed before its departure. So the charging blocks effectively add energy after the charging slots.\n- The UAV is free to depart for next mission starting at slot s+T+1 (if C=0, after turnaround, it's free). Actually if C=0, then turnaround ends after slot s+T, so earliest next departure is s+T+1. If C>0, then charging occupies slots s+T+1 to s+T+C, so earliest next departure is s+T+C+1.\n\nWe must also respect pad capacity at origin and destination. Each vertiport has pad_capacity 2. A UAV occupies a pad during departure and arrival? Typically, a pad is needed for takeoff and landing. So at departure slot, the origin vertiport must have a free pad. During the flight, the UAV does not occupy a pad. At arrival, at the destination, it occupies a pad during the arrival slot? Possibly the arrival slot itself consumes a pad. Since the flight ends at slot s+T-1, the UAV lands at that slot, so it needs a pad at destination at slot s+T-1. Then turnaround occupies a pad at destination for the turnaround slot s+T. And charging might also require a charger and pad? Charger capacity: each charger can charge one UAV at a time. The UAV occupies a charger during its post-service charge blocks. It does not need a pad while charging? Actually, a UAV on the ground is on a pad. So pad capacity must be respected for all ground slots. So at the destination, from arrival slot through turnaround and charging slots, the UAV occupies a pad. So we need to ensure that the number of UAVs at a vertiport at each slot does not exceed pad capacity (2). Similarly, at the origin, departure slot occupies a pad, and also if it is starting from there after previous charging, the UAV might be on the pad before departure. But typically, the pad is needed for takeoff/landing and ground time. So we need to track ground presence.\n\nAlso corridor capacity: each corridor has capacity per slot (default 2, except COR_08 slot 4 capacity 1 due to override). A flight using a corridor occupies that corridor for all slots of the flight. For a path with travel_slots T, the corridor is occupied during the departure slot and subsequent T-1 slots? Probably it occupies the corridor from slot s to s+T-1. So corridor capacity applies for each of those slots.\n\nAlso closures: corridors can be closed at certain slots. If a flight uses a closed corridor at any slot of its travel, it's forbidden. So PATH_08 (COR_10) closed from slot 7 to 30, so any departure slot >=7 will conflict because flight slots would include 7 or later. Since MISSION_04 release is 7, earliest departure 7, it would be prohibited. So PATH_08 is unavailable.\n\nCharger capacity: default 2 chargers at each vertiport. But intervention has charger_capacity_overrides empty list, so default of 2 applies. So at most 2 UAVs can be charging simultaneously at each vertiport.\n\nNow we must compute for each UAV the feasible schedule that satisfies all constraints and optimizes lexicographically: urgent holds (number of urgent missions held?), total holds (number of missions held), repair distance (probably total distance flown?), weighted tardiness (probably sum of tardiness weighting?), delay plus charge wait, then energy. But the task description says: \"Optimize the stated task lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\" I need to understand what each objective means. Since the baseline decisions include all missions not held, and some departure slots and charge_blocks, maybe we need to possibly hold missions? The decisions have \"held\": false for all, but the task allows held missions: \"If held is true, path_id and depart_slot must be null and charge_blocks must be 0.\" So we can hold missions. The prioritization: urgent holds (maybe number of missions with priority \"urgent\" that are held? All missions here are \"routine\", so urgent holds is 0 regardless. So that objective is 0 anyway. Then total holds: we want to minimize the number of held missions. So we should try to hold as few as possible, ideally 0. Then repair distance: minimize total distance flown (sum of distances of non-held missions). Then weighted tardiness: minimize sum of tardiness weights? What is the weight? Could be linked to priority? \"weighted tardiness\" might be the sum of (tardiness) * weight, where weight is maybe 1 for routine, higher for urgent? Not specified, but we can infer from typical usage: maybe each mission has a due slot, and tardiness is max(0, completion_slot - due_slot) where completion is arrival? Or maybe delivery completion? But it says \"weighted tardiness\". We need to minimize it. Then \"delay plus charge wait\": probably the sum of (departure_slot - release_slot) plus the total charge wait time? Or maybe it's the total delay (departure delay) plus total charge wait (time spent waiting for charger)? Not entirely clear. Then \"energy\": total energy consumption.\n\nGiven that we are repairing a candidate baseline, we need to produce a new set of decisions. The baseline had: \nMISSION_01: UAV_01, path PATH_11, depart slot 4, charge 0\nMISSION_02: UAV_03, path PATH_05, depart slot 4, charge 0\nMISSION_03: UAV_01, path PATH_01, depart slot 7, charge 0\nMISSION_04: UAV_03, path PATH_08, depart slot 7, charge 0\nMISSION_05: UAV_03, path PATH_10, depart slot 15, charge 0\nMISSION_06: UAV_02, path PATH_03, depart slot 20, charge 0\n\nThis baseline is infeasible because of corridor closures: PATH_11 uses COR_02 which is closed slots 4-27, so MISSION_01 cannot use PATH_11. Also PATH_08 uses COR_10 closed slots 7-30, so MISSION_04 cannot use PATH_08. And also there might be energy issues? Need to check.\n\nOur task: produce a feasible repair, respecting all constraints, and optimize lexicographically.\n\nFirst, let's list all missions and their possible paths and times.\n\nMISSION_01: UAV_01, release 4, due 10. From VP_04 to VP_03.\nOptions:\n- PATH_02: distance 0.9, energy 0.1359, wind 0, travel 2 slots, corridor COR_09. (COR_09 not closed, capacity 2 default). Depart slot >=4. Arrival slot = depart + 2 - 1 = depart+1? Actually if travel 2 slots, it occupies slots s and s+1. So arrival at end of s+1. Then turnaround s+2, earliest next departure s+3.\n- PATH_06: distance 1.06, energy 0.170464, wind 1, travel 2, corridor COR_08. COR_08 capacity override: at slot 4 capacity 1, other slots? Only slot 4 mentioned. So if we depart at slot 4, COR_08 capacity is 1. If we depart later, say slot 5, we need to check if there's override for slot 5? The override only lists slot 4: {\"capacity\":1,\"corridor_id\":\"COR_08\",\"slot\":4}. For other slots, default capacity 2 applies. So if we depart at slot 5, COR_08 capacity is 2. But also note that MISSION_02 also uses COR_08 (PATH_05) and its release is 4. So they might conflict.\n- PATH_07: distance 1.31, energy 0.19781, wind 0, travel 2, corridor COR_11. COR_11 default capacity 2, no closures.\n- PATH_11: not available due to COR_02 closed.\n\nMISSION_02: UAV_03, release 4, due 6. VP_04 to VP_03. Only PATH_05: distance 1.06, energy 0.132956, wind 1, travel 2, corridor COR_08. Due 6 means it must arrive by slot 6? Or due slot 6 is the latest completion? The due slot likely is the latest allowed arrival slot? Possibly the delivery must be completed by the end of due slot. Since there's no explicit completion definition, we assume the mission's flight must arrive on or before due slot. So arrival slot = depart + travel_slots - 1 <= due_slot. So depart + 1 <= 6 => depart <= 5. So earliest depart 4, can't depart 6 because arrival would be 7 > 6. So feasible depart slots: 4, 5. Also note that turnaround after arrival will be at slot depart+2, which is fine. But due only constraints arrival, not turnaround. So MISSION_02 must depart at 4 or 5. With depart 4, arrival slots 4-5? Actually travel 2 slots: depart 4 means flight occupies slots 4 and 5, arrival at end of 5, so arrival slot is 5. That's <=6, OK. Depart 5: flight 5-6, arrival 6, OK. So both feasible.\n\nMISSION_03: UAV_01, release 7, due 22. VP_03 to VP_02. PATH_01: distance 0.8, energy 0.128652, wind 1, travel 1 slot. Travel 1 slot means flight occupies exactly one slot? So depart s, arrival same slot s? That seems odd: if travel_slots=1, the duration is 1 slot. Usually it would occupy just that slot. So arrival at end of that slot? Then turnaround would be next slot. So if depart s, the flight is in corridor during slot s, arrival at end of s. Then turnaround slot s+1. So earliest next depart s+2. Due 22: arrival slot s <= 22. So s <= 22. Also release 7, so s >= 7. So broad range.\n\nMISSION_04: UAV_03, release 7, due 24. VP_03 to VP_05.\nOptions:\n- PATH_04: distance 4.0, energy 0.50172, wind 1, travel 2, corridor COR_04.\n- PATH_09: distance 1.0, energy 0.113, wind 0, travel 2, corridor COR_03.\n- PATH_08: not available.\nDue 24: arrival slot = depart+1 <= 24 => depart <= 23. Release 7.\nSo both feasible.\n\nMISSION_05: UAV_03, release 15, due 27. VP_05 to VP_06. PATH_10: distance 3.15, energy 0.35595, wind 0, travel 1 slot. Travel 1 slot: flight occupies 1 slot. Arrival slot = depart. So due 27 => depart <= 27. Release 15. So depart 15 to 27 feasible.\n\nMISSION_06: UAV_02, release 20, due 27. VP_04 to VP_01. PATH_03: distance 1.0, energy 0.151, wind 0, travel 2 slots. Arrival = depart+1 <= 27 => depart <= 26. Release 20, so depart 20 to 26.\n\nNow, UAV initial conditions:\nUAV_01: start at VP_04, initial SOC overridden to 2.731181 kWh. Battery capacity 22, reserve 2.5. Available slot 4. So at slot 4, UAV_01 is at VP_04 with SOC 2.731181 kWh. It must fly MISSION_01 from VP_04 to VP_03. Need to have enough energy: incoming deadhead none, so we deduct service flight energy from initial SOC. So required: SOC - flight_energy >= reserve_kwh. For each path:\n- PATH_02: energy 0.1359 -> remaining 2.731181 - 0.1359 = 2.595281, which is > 2.5. OK.\n- PATH_06: 0.170464 -> remaining 2.560717, >2.5. OK.\n- PATH_07: 0.19781 -> remaining 2.533371, >2.5 (but 2.533371 - 2.5 = 0.033371, very close but okay).\nSo energy feasible for all options. However, we must also consider if there is any charging? charge_blocks is a decision; we can set charge_blocks for MISSION_01 to 0 or up to max 3. That charge will occur at VP_03 after MISSION_01 and its turnaround. It can be used for later missions of UAV_01 (MISSION_03). So we might want to charge to ensure enough energy for MISSION_03.\n\nMISSION_03 departs from VP_03, distance 0.8, energy 0.128652. We need to check SOC before MISSION_03 departure. SOC after MISSION_01 flight, minus reserve check, plus any charging from MISSION_01's post-service charge. The charging blocks add 0.95 kWh per block for UAV_01 (charge_kwh_per_block: 0.95). So if we charge C blocks, SOC before MISSION_03 departure = SOC after MISSION_01 (after reserve check) + C * 0.95, capped at battery capacity 22. However, the reserve check is done after flight energy deduction, and the SOC after flight must be >= reserve. The charging then increases SOC. At the start of next mission, we can use that. For UAV_01, after MISSION_01 flight: SOC_after = initial_SOC - flight_energy. This must be >= 2.5. Then charging adds. For MISSION_03, we need SOC_before_M03 = min(22, SOC_after + charge_blocks*0.95). Then we deduct MISSION_03 flight energy, and after that must be >= reserve (2.5). So SOC_before_M03 - 0.128652 >= 2.5. So SOC_before_M03 >= 2.628652. So we can check if SOC_after >= 2.628652, then no charge needed. If SOC_after is lower, we need charging.\n\nFor PATH_02: SOC_after = 2.731181 - 0.1359 = 2.595281, which is less than 2.628652. So we need charging to reach at least 2.628652. Charging adds 0.95 per block. With 1 block: SOC = 2.595281 + 0.95 = 3.545281, which is > 2.628652 and also < 22. So with 1 charge block we can do MISSION_03. But also check that charging doesn't exceed capacity? With 1 block, SOC becomes 3.545, fine. With 0 blocks, SOC is 2.595, which is less than required, so MISSION_03 would be impossible (would violate reserve after flight). So MISSION_01 must have at least 1 charge block if using PATH_02. For PATH_06: SOC_after = 2.560717, also less than 2.628652, need at least 1 charge block. With 1 block: 2.560717+0.95=3.510717, enough. For PATH_07: SOC_after = 2.533371, need at least 1 block (2.533371+0.95=3.483371). So in all cases, UAV_01 needs at least one charge block after MISSION_01 to do MISSION_03. It could also charge more if beneficial for energy objective (maybe we want to minimize energy usage? Energy is last objective, so we might not care as much, but we still need to set a feasible charge_blocks, possibly higher if it helps other objectives? But higher charging might consume charger capacity and cause delays? But we need to minimize delay plus charge wait. Could be that longer charging means later departure for MISSION_03? But MISSION_03 has release 7 and due 22. So MISSION_03 can depart later. However, charging takes 1 slot per block. So if we charge 1 block, MISSION_01: depart slot s (>=4), arrive s+1, turnaround s+2, charge block s+3, then UAV_01 is free at s+4. So MISSION_03 earliest departure could be s+4. MISSION_03 release is 7, so if s+4 < 7, we may still need to wait until 7. But if we depart MISSION_01 at 4, then s=4: arrival 5, turnaround 6, charge 7, free at 8. So MISSION_03 earliest departure 8. That's fine. If we depart at 5: free at 9. So charging doesn't cause extra delay compared to no charge because even with 0 charge, free at s+2 (turnaround ends), which for s=4 is 6, but MISSION_03 release is 7, so earliest departure is 7 anyway. So for MISSION_01 depart 4, with 0 charge you'd be free at 6, but must wait until 7 for release, so you can depart at 7 anyway. With 1 charge, free at 8, so departure might be 8, which is later. That could affect tardiness. MISSION_03 due 22, so departure up to 22 is okay? Actually arrival slot = depart (since travel 1) so arrival = depart slot. Due 22 means arrival <=22, so depart <=22. So delay is not an issue. But weighted tardiness might be calculated based on arrival vs due? If arrival is after due, tardiness = arrival - due. So we can avoid tardiness by departing by 22. So as long as depart <=22, no tardiness. So charging that delays to 8 or 9 doesn't cause tardiness. But \"delay plus charge wait\" might penalize departure delay relative to release, and also any wait for charger? Not sure. Let's assume delay is departure_slot - release_slot. So for MISSION_03, delay = depart - 7. For depart 8, delay=1; depart 7, delay=0. So minimizing delay would favor earlier departure. So we might prefer to depart MISSION_03 at 7 if possible. That would require UAV_01 to be free by slot 7. With MISSION_01 depart 4 and charge 0, free at 6, depart at 7, delay 0. But with charge 0, energy insufficient. So we need charge. If we depart MISSION_01 at 4 and charge 1, free at 8, so MISSION_03 earliest depart 8 (delay 1). Could we depart MISSION_01 at 4 with charge 0 but then charge after MISSION_03? No, MISSION_03 has its own charge_blocks after it, but those cannot help MISSION_03 itself because charge is post-service. So we must charge after MISSION_01 to have energy for MISSION_03. So we need at least 1 charge block, causing at least delay of 1 for MISSION_03 if MISSION_01 departs at 4. If we depart MISSION_01 earlier? The release is 4, so earliest depart is 4. So delay for MISSION_03 will be at least 1 if we need charge. Could we depart MISSION_01 at 4, charge 1, but then MISSION_03 depart at 8? That's delay 1. Or we could depart MISSION_01 at 5, charge 1: free at 9, depart 9, delay 2. So better to depart MISSION_01 as early as possible to minimize delay.\n\nUAV_03: initial SOC 9.635464 kWh, capacity 16, reserve 1.5. Start at VP_04, available slot 4. Missions: MISSION_02, MISSION_04, MISSION_05. MISSION_02 flies VP_04->VP_03, energy 0.132956 kWh. Then MISSION_04 VP_03->VP_05, energy either 0.50172 (PATH_04) or 0.113 (PATH_09). Then MISSION_05 VP_05->VP_06, energy 0.35595. Need to ensure SOC after each flight >= 1.5. Also we can charge after each mission. UAV_03 charge rate 1.2 kWh per block (charge_kwh_per_block = 1.2). Battery capacity 16.\n\nLet's compute.\nFirst, MISSION_02: start SOC 9.635464. Flight energy 0.132956, remaining = 9.502508, >1.5. Good. Then after turnaround, we can charge. If we charge 0 blocks, MISSION_04 will start with SOC 9.502508. MISSION_04 energy: if PATH_09 (0.113), remaining = 9.389508, still >1.5. Then MISSION_05: start 9.389508, flight 0.35595, remaining 9.033558, >1.5. So with PATH_09, no charge needed at all. If we use PATH_04 (0.50172), then after MISSION_04: SOC = 9.502508 - 0.50172 = 9.000788, >1.5. Then MISSION_05: 9.000788 - 0.35595 = 8.644838, >1.5. So also no charge needed. So UAV_03 can complete all missions without any charging, assuming SOC margin is fine. So charge_blocks can be 0 for all UAV_03 missions.\n\nUAV_02: initial SOC 8.5, capacity 22, reserve 2.5. MISSION_06: energy 0.151, after flight SOC = 8.349, >2.5. No charge needed. So charge_blocks 0.\n\nNow constraints: corridor capacity and closures, pad capacity.\n\nWe need to assign paths and departure slots to all missions, possibly hold some. Since we want to minimize holds (0 holds preferable), we try to schedule all missions feasibly.\n\nWe have three UAVs. Each UAV's sequence is independent, but they share resources (corridors, pads, chargers) at vertiports. So conflict may arise for corridor COR_08 at slot 4, and pad capacity at VP_04 and VP_03.\n\nFirst, UAV_02 only has MISSION_06, departing VP_04 at slot >=20. That probably won't conflict with earlier missions of others. UAV_01 and UAV_03 both start at VP_04 at slot 4. They both need to depart from VP_04 on their first missions: MISSION_01 and MISSION_02. So at departure slot, each needs a pad at VP_04. VP_04 pad capacity is 2. So both can depart simultaneously if slots align, because there are 2 pads. So no conflict.\n\nBut both may want to use COR_08 at slot 4. MISSION_02 must use PATH_0