s on the ground at a vertiport from the moment it lands (including arrival slot) until it departs for its next mission, occupying the vertiport's pad. So during turnaround and charging, it occupies a pad. If it has no next mission (idle), it remains at the last vertiport, occupying a pad indefinitely. So we need to prevent exceeding pad capacity.\n\nAt start: UAV_01, UAV_02, UAV_03 are all at VP_04 from slot 0? They become available at 4. But they must have arrived somehow. Since no incoming deadhead, they are just there. For pad capacity, we should consider them as occupying pads from slot 0 until they depart. So at slot 0-3, three UAVs at VP_04 exceeds capacity 2. That would be infeasible. The baseline might ignore this because they only consider slots from 4 onward? The slot_min:5 might indicate that slots 0-4 are not binding? Actually slot_min:5 could mean that the horizon slots of interest start at 5? That seems odd. Let's look at the instance: horizon_slots:32, slot_min:5. Possibly slots are 0 to 31, and slot_min is the minimum slot number for scheduling? But the interventions mention slot 4. I think it's safer to assume that pad capacity is only enforced from slot 4 onward, or the initial occupancy before first available slot is not counted. Many scheduling problems consider that the UAVs are in a \"ready\" state without occupying pads until they start their missions. To be pragmatic, I'll assume the pad capacity only applies during the execution of missions (turnaround, charging, and flight landing/takeoff). Since the baseline has MISSION_01 and MISSION_02 both depart at 4, and UAV_02 remains until 20, if we treat UAV_02 as occupying a pad from slot 0-19, that's 1 UAV on pad, which is fine. At slot 4, both UAV_01 and UAV_03 depart, so only UAV_02 remains, still 1 pad. So no pad capacity violation. So as long as we don't have more than 2 UAVs on the ground simultaneously at a vertiport, we are fine. The initial three at VP_04 would only be a problem if they all stay beyond slot 4, but they don't. So we can assume that before slot 4, they were not yet \"available\" and thus not occupying pads? Anyway, we'll schedule so that after slot 4, we respect pad capacity.\n\nNow, schedule UAV_01: MISSION_01 then MISSION_03.\nPlan: MISSION_01 depart 4, path PATH_02, travel 2: flight slots 4-5, arrival VP_03 end of slot 5. Turnaround slot 6. Then post-service charge: we need at least 1 block. If we charge 1 block, it occupies slot 7 (charger at VP_03). UAV_01 free at slot 8. MISSION_03: release 7, so earliest depart 8. We'll set depart 8. PATH_01 travel 1 slot: flight slot 8, arrival VP_02 end of 8? Travel 1 slot: if depart 8, flight occupies slot 8 only? Then arrival end of slot 8. Turnaround slot 9. Charge? Not needed. So free at slot 10. But no more missions, ok.\nCheck energy: MISSION_01 start 2.731181, flight 0.1359, remaining 2.595281 >= 2.5 OK. Charge 1 block: SOC becomes min(22, 2.595281+0.95)=3.545281. MISSION_03 depart with 3.545281, flight 0.128652, remaining 3.416629 >= 2.5 OK.\nCorridors: MISSION_01 PATH_02 uses COR_09 slots 4,5. COR_09 capacity 2, no closures, fine. MISSION_03 PATH_01 uses COR_05 slot 8 (since travel 1, only slot 8). COR_05 capacity 2, fine.\nPads: Depart VP_04 slot 4: UAV_01 on pad. At same time, UAV_03 may also depart VP_04 slot 4 or 5. VP_04 pad capacity 2, so two departures at same slot is fine. After departure, UAV_01 leaves pad. Arrival VP_03 slot 5: UAV_01 lands, occupies pad. Turnaround slot 6: pad. Charge slot 7: still on pad, also uses charger. VP_03 pad capacity 2. Any other UAV at VP_03? UAV_03 will also arrive at VP_03 after MISSION_02. Need to check VP_03 pad capacity.\n\nNow UAV_03: MISSION_02, MISSION_04, MISSION_05.\nMISSION_02: PATH_05, COR_08, travel 2. Depart slot? Option 4 or 5. If we depart at 4: flight 4-5, arrival VP_03 end of 5. Turnaround 6. Charge? None needed. Then MISSION_04: from VP_03 to VP_05. Release 7. So after turnaround, free at slot 7 (since turnaround occupies 6, next availability 7). But MISSION_04 release 7, so can depart at 7. So if MISSION_02 departs 4, UAV_03 arrives VP_03 at end of 5, turnaround 6, free at 7, MISSION_04 depart 7. That works. If MISSION_02 departs 5: flight 5-6, arrival 6, turnaround 7, free at 8, then MISSION_04 depart 8. That would delay MISSION_04. So better to depart MISSION_02 at 4 to free up earlier and possibly reduce delay? But MISSION_04 due 24, no rush. Still, to minimize \"delay plus charge wait\", we'd want to depart as early as possible to reduce delay (departure - release). So MISSION_02 depart 4 is better (delay 0). MISSION_04 release 7, depart 7 (delay 0) if possible. So we'll try for MISSION_02 depart 4, MISSION_04 depart 7.\n\nBut we must check corridor capacity: MISSION_02 uses COR_08 at slot 4 and 5. MISSION_01 we chose PATH_02 (COR_09), so no conflict. Also, MISSION_04: from VP_03 to VP_05. We can choose PATH_09 (COR_03) distance 1.0, energy 0.113, travel 2 slots, no closure. So depart 7, flight slots 7-8, arrival VP_05 end of 8. Turnaround 9. Then MISSION_05: from VP_05 to VP_06, release 15, travel 1 slot, so earliest depart 15? But after turnaround at 9, UAV_03 is free at 10. So could depart at 15 or later. If we depart MISSION_05 at 15, delay 0. So schedule: MISSION_04 depart 7, arrive VP_05 at 8, turnaround 9, free at 10. MISSION_05 depart 15, flight 15, arrive VP_06 end of 15? travel 1 slot, flight slot 15, arrival 15, turnaround 16, free 17. That works.\n\nCheck pad capacity at VP_03: After MISSION_01, UAV_01 arrives VP_03 end of slot 5, occupies pad from 5 (arrival), 6 turnaround, 7 charging. So it's on pad at VP_03 at slots 5,6,7. Also UAV_03 after MISSION_02 arrives VP_03 end of 5, pad slots 5,6 (arrival and turnaround), departs at 7 (so slot 7 it is departing, so it occupies pad at slot 7 for takeoff? Typically departure slot the UAV is on the pad before takeoff. So at slot 7, UAV_03 would be on pad at VP_03 for departure. So at slot 5: both UAV_01 and UAV_03 arrive, so 2 pads occupied, ok. Slot 6: UAV_01 turnaround, UAV_03 turnaround, 2 pads, ok. Slot 7: UAV_01 charging (needs pad and charger), UAV_03 departing (needs pad). That's 2 pads, still ok. So pad capacity at VP_03 is fine, with 2 pads exactly. Charger capacity at VP_03: UAV_01 charging at slot 7. Chargers: 2. So fine.\n\nNow UAV_02: MISSION_06 depart 20, PATH_03 travel 2: flight slots 20-21, arrival VP_01 end of 21. Turnaround 22. No further missions. Check pad at VP_04: UAV_02 is at VP_04 from start, but since not flying until slot 20, it might occupy a pad from slot 0 to 19 at VP_04. VP_04 pad capacity 2. During this period, we have UAV_01 depart at 4, UAV_03 depart at 4, so after slot 4, only UAV_02 is at VP_04, occupying 1 pad, ok. Before slot 4, all three were there but we assume it's allowed. So no problem. At slot 20, UAV_02 departs, so pad free.\n\nNow, check corridor capacities for all corridors used:\n- COR_09: MISSION_01 slots 4,5 (capacity 2, fine)\n- COR_08: MISSION_02 slots 4,5 (capacity 2 for slot 5? override only slot 4 capacity 1, but MISSION_02 is the only user of COR_08 at slot 4, so capacity 1 is sufficient. At slot 5, capacity 2, fine).\n- COR_05: MISSION_03 slot 8 (capacity 2)\n- COR_03: MISSION_04 slots 7,8 (capacity 2)\n- COR_06: MISSION_05 slot 15 (capacity 2)\n- COR_01: MISSION_06 slots 20,21 (capacity 2)\n\nNo closures violated.\n\nNow, check due dates:\nMISSION_01: arrival end of slot 5 <= 10 ok.\nMISSION_02: arrival end of 5 <= 6 ok.\nMISSION_03: arrival slot 8 <= 22 ok (travel 1, arrival = depart = 8).\nMISSION_04: arrival end of 8 <= 24 ok.\nMISSION_05: arrival slot 15 <= 27 ok.\nMISSION_06: arrival end of 21 <= 27 ok.\n\nNo holds needed.\n\nNow, we need to verify the energy at each stage with the chosen charge_blocks. We already did. UAV_01 charge 1 block, others 0.\n\nNow, what about the objective \"delay plus charge wait\"? Maybe we need to compute total delay = sum(max(0, depart_slot - release_slot))? For all missions: MISSION_01 depart 4, release 4, delay 0. MISSION_02 depart 4, release 4, delay 0. MISSION_03 release 7, depart 8, delay 1. MISSION_04 release 7, depart 7, delay 0. MISSION_05 release 15, depart 15, delay 0. MISSION_06 release 20, depart 20, delay 0. So total delay = 1. Could we reduce delay for MISSION_03? It requires charge. If we could depart MISSION_01 earlier? Not possible. If we use a path for MISSION_01 that requires no charge? But all paths require at least 1 charge block given initial SOC 2.731181 and MISSION_03 energy need. Let's double-check if there is any path with lower energy that might allow MISSION_03 without charge. MISSION_03 energy 0.128652, reserve 2.5, so minimum SOC before MISSION_03 is 2.628652. After MISSION_01 flight, SOC_after = 2.731181 - flight_energy. For SOC_after >= 2.628652, we need flight_energy <= 2.731181 - 2.628652 = 0.102529. The smallest flight energy among available paths is PATH_02: 0.1359 > 0.1025. So impossible without charging. So at least 1 charge block is needed. With 1 charge block, we add 0.95, so SOC becomes SOC_after + 0.95, which is always > required. So the earliest MISSION_03 can depart is after turnaround and charge. MISSION_01 depart 4, turn 6, charge 7, free 8. So depart 8 is earliest. Could we reduce turnaround? No, turnaround_slots=1 fixed. Could we charge less than 1 block? Charge_blocks must be integer, 0 or 1+. So 1 is minimum. So delay of 1 for MISSION_03 is unavoidable. So total delay = 1. The \"charge wait\" might refer to waiting for a charger? We didn't have to wait for charger because only one UAV charging at VP_03 at slot 7. So charge wait = 0. So total delay plus charge wait = 1.\n\nNow, repair distance: sum of distances of chosen paths. PATH_02: 0.9, PATH_05: 1.06, PATH_01: 0.8, PATH_09: 1.0, PATH_10: 3.15, PATH_03: 1.0. Total distance = 0.9 + 1.06 + 0.8 + 1.0 + 3.15 + 1.0 = 7.91. Could we reduce distance by choosing different path for MISSION_04? PATH_04 distance 4.0 is larger, so PATH_09 (1.0) is better. For MISSION_01, PATH_02 distance 0.9 is smallest. So total distance 7.91 is minimal given the paths.\n\nWeighted tardiness: all missions arrive on time, so tardiness 0. So weighted tardiness = 0.\n\nEnergy consumption: sum of energy consumed. PATH_02: 0.1359, PATH_05: 0.132956, PATH_01: 0.128652, PATH_09: 0.113, PATH_10: 0.35595, PATH_03: 0.151. Total = 0.1359+0.132956=0.268856; +0.128652=0.397508; +0.113=0.510508; +0.35595=0.866458; +0.151=1.017458 kWh. So total energy = 1.017458 kWh. (Note: charging adds energy, but energy cost is about consumption? The objective \"energy\" likely means total energy used from battery, i.e., sum of flight energies. So we minimize total energy consumption. This is the smallest since we chose shortest distances which generally correlate with energy.)\n\nNow, we must also check if any other constraints like max charge_blocks 3. We used 1, fine.\n\nNow, we need to confirm that the post-service charging does not violate energy upper bound: \"Check the battery upper bound immediately after post-service charging.\" That is, after charging, SOC must not exceed battery capacity. For UAV_01, after charge 1 block: 2.595281+0.95=3.545281 ≤ 22, ok.\n\nAlso check that the reserve is checked after service flight before charging. For MISSION_01: 2.731181-0.1359=2.595281≥2.5 ok. For MISSION_03: 3.545281-0.128652=3.416629≥2.5 ok.\n\nNow, is there any issue with pad capacity at VP_05 for MISSION_04 arrival? MISSION_04 uses PATH_09, which has travel 2 slots, arrive VP_05 end of 8, turnaround 9. At VP_05, any other UAVs? Only UAV_03 will be there. So only one UAV on pad at VP_05 at slots 8,9. Capacity 2, fine. Then UAV_03 remains there until MISSION_05 departure at 15. So from slot 10 to 14, UAV_03 is idle at VP_05, occupying a pad. At slot 15, it departs. No other UAV at VP_05, so pad usage 1, fine.\n\nCharger usage: only UAV_01 at VP_03 slot 7. No other charging, so charger capacity fine.\n\nNow, check corridor capacity for COR_03 (MISSION_04): slots 7,8. No other flights using COR_03. Fine.\n\nNow, what about COR_10 closure? Not used.\n\nNow, we need to ensure that the departure slots respect the \"incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge\" order. Since no incoming deadheads, we just need to ensure that the service flight departure slot is after the UAV is available, and after previous mission's turnaround/charge. We have that.\n\nNow, the baseline had MISSION_03 depart 7, but we moved to 8. That's a change.\n\nNow, we need to output decisions for all missions. Held false for all.\n\nSo decisions:\n- MISSION_01: uav_id UAV_01, held false, path_id PATH_02, depart_slot 4, charge_blocks 1\n- MISSION_02: uav_id UAV_03, held false, path_id PATH_05, depart_slot 4, charge_blocks 0\n- MISSION_03: uav_id UAV_01, held false, path_id PATH_01, depart_slot 8, charge_blocks 0\n- MISSION_04: uav_id UAV_03, held false, path_id PATH_09, depart_slot 7, charge_blocks 0\n- MISSION_05: uav_id UAV_03, held false, path_id PATH_10, depart_slot 15, charge_blocks 0\n- MISSION_06: uav_id UAV_02, held false, path_id PATH_03, depart_slot 20, charge_blocks 0\n\nBut we need to double-check if MISSION_04 depart 7 is feasible with MISSION_02 depart 4. MISSION_02 arrival VP_03 end of 5, turnaround 6, so UAV_03 is free at 7. So it can depart at 7. But the departure slot 7 for MISSION_04: the flight uses corridor from slot 7 and 8. So it needs to be at VP_03, and the pad at VP_03 must be available. At slot 7, UAV_01 is charging at VP_03 (on pad), UAV_03 is departing (on pad). So two pads used, capacity 2. That's ok. However, the pad at VP_03 for slot 7: UAV_01 occupies pad for charging. Does a UAV charging need to remain on a pad? Yes, a charging UAV is on the ground, so it uses a pad. So both pads occupied. That's allowed. So no issue.\n\nBut wait: UAV_01's charge block at slot 7: we assumed it starts at slot 7. The order: arrival at 5, turnaround at 6, then post-service charge starts at 7. Yes, slot 7 is the first charge block. So it's charging at slot 7. So pad occupied. At the same slot, UAV_03 departs from VP_03. So both pads occupied. Pad capacity 2, fine.\n\nNow, what about the charger? UAV_01 uses one charger at slot 7. There is no other charging, so charger capacity ok.\n\nNow, check if UAV_03's MISSION_02 arrival at VP_03 at slot 5: does it need a pad? Yes, it lands at slot 5, so it occupies a pad for arrival. UAV_01 also arrives at slot 5? MISSION_01 travel 2 slots: depart 4, flight slots 4 and 5, so it arrives at end of slot 5. So both arrive at slot 5. So at slot 5, two arrivals, both need pads. That's 2 pads, ok. Then turnaround at slot 6: both need pads. That's 2 pads, ok.\n\nSo all pad constraints satisfied.\n\nNow, any other closed corridors? COR_02 closed, not used. COR_10 closed, not used.\n\nNow, we must also consider if there is any incoming deadhead? All null, so none.\n\nNow, check UAV_01 initial SOC override: 2.731181, we used that.\n\nNow, is there any requirement about the mission order? We kept order.\n\nNow, what about the \"delay plus charge wait\" objective? Maybe there is an additional \"charge wait\" if a UAV has to wait for a charger. Here, UAV_01 charges at slot 7, and no other charging, so charge wait 0. So total delay+chargewait = 1.\n\nCould we have MISSION_03 depart at 7 by using a different charging arrangement? Perhaps we could charge after MISSION_03 instead? No, because MISSION_03 needs energy before its flight. The only way to get energy for MISSION_03 is to charge after MISSION_01 (since MISSION_01's destination is VP_03, same as MISSION_03's origin). So no. Could we use a different path for MISSION_01 that allows MISSION_03 to depart at 7? No, because MISSION_01 would still need turnaround and charge, taking slots 6 and 7. The earliest free is 8. If we could do turnaround and charge in parallel? No, they are sequential: turnaround must finish before charge starts. So 6 for turnaround, then charge block 7, free 8. So 7 is not possible.\n\nThus, our solution seems optimal.\n\nNow, we must output JSON exactly.\n\n{\"record_id\":\"CA12_CORE_026\",\"decisions\":[{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_01\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_02\",\"depart_slot\":4,\"charge_blocks\":1},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_02\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_05\",\"depart_slot\":4,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_03\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_01\",\"depart_slot\":8,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_04\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_09\",\"depart_slot\":7,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_05\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_10\",\"depart_slot\":15,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_06\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_02\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_03\",\"depart_slot\":20,\"charge_blocks\":0}]}\n\nBut note: the order of missions in decisions array doesn't matter as long as all are present.\n\nWe should double-check the exact spelling of IDs: mission_id \"MISSION_01\", etc. uav_id \"UAV_01\", etc. path_id \"PATH_02\", etc. All from given data.\n\nCheck baseline had MISSION_03 depart 7, but we changed to 8. That's fine.\n\nNow, is there any issue with the slot numbers? depart_slot 4, 7, 8, 15, 20. All within horizon 32. Depart slot 8 for MISSION_03 is fine.\n\nNow, confirm that UAV_01 after MISSION_01 charging, the SOC is used for MISSION_03. MISSION_03 depart at 8, so the charge block at slot 7 is complete before departure at 8. Energy from charging is available at start of later mission. Yes.\n\nNow, verify that the \"charge_blocks\" attached to a mission occurs at that mission's destination. For MISSION_01, destination VP_03, charge block at slot 7. So it's correct.\n\nNow, check that UAV_03's MISSION_05 depart 15 is after release 15, ok. It departs at 15, flight takes 1 slot, so arrival at 15, due 27 ok.\n\nNow, final check: The interv